              ext_lo - Set IF external-LO (DBBC3 racks)

  Syntax:   ext_lo=loX,freq,sb,lo_min,lo_max,lo_sb
            ext_lo=
            ext_lo=loX
            ext_lo=all

  Response: ext_lo/loX,freq,sb,lo_min,lo_max,lo_sb

Settable parameters:

  loX       LO (IF) for this external-LO: 'loa', 'lob', ..., up to the last IF
            in the system ('loh' is the last possible) or 'all'. The default
            is 'all'. No default. No previous value ('*') available.

  freq      External-LO frequency for this IF, MHz. Must be positive. No
            default. No previous value ('*') available.

  sb        External-LO side-band for this channel, 'usb' or 'lsb'. No
            default. No previous value ('*') available.

  lo_min    Minimum LO frequency (from 'lo=...) that this external-LO applies
            to. Must be positive. Default is no minimum. No previous value
            ('*') available.

  lo_max    Maximum LO frequency (from 'lo=...) that this external-LO applies
            to. Can't be set if 'lo_min' is not set. Must be positive.  Must
            be greater or equal to 'lo_min'. Default is 'lo_min'.  No previous
            value ('*') available.

  lo_sb     LO side-band (from 'lo=...) that this external-LO applies to,
            'usb', 'lsb', or 'all'. Default is 'all'. No previous value ('*')
            available.

Monitor-only Parameters: None.

Comments:
This command specifies the total external-LO, before the DBBC3, for a given
IF. The total LO before the BBCs is still given by the 'lo=...' commands.
This command does not set the external-LO; it just describes what is in use.
The external-LO is used by the 'synth=...' command to calculate the
synthesizer frequency needed to achieve the total LO if the corresponding
'ifX' input is '2'.

Before defining the external-LOs for a mode, an initial,'ext_lo=' (no
parameters) should be used to delete any previous defined entries.

The external-LOs can be defined explicitly by IF. For example:

    ext_lo=
    ext_lo=loa,10000,usb
    ext_lo=lob,20000,lsb

For more complicated situations, a table of external-LO values that apply
depending on the total LO for the IF can be used. The goal, when it is
possible, is to allow stations to specify a table of values that does not need
to be revised depending on which input signal is connected, and where. For
example:

    ext_lo=
    ext_lo=loa,10000,usb,15000,16000
    ext_lo=loa,20000,usb,25000,26600

The above two statements would specify 10000 as the external-LO if the total
LO is between 15000 and 16000 and 20000 if between 25000 and 26000.

If 'lo_min' and 'lo_max' are null, the table entry applies for all total LO
frequencies for that IF. If 'lo_sb' is null or 'all', it applies to a total LO
for either side-band. If 'loX' is null or 'all', the entry applies
(conditionally, based on LO parameters) to all IFs.

Generally speaking, it is recommended to make the 'lo_min', 'lo_max', and
'lo_sb' parameters as restrictive as possible to reduce the risk of having a
table entry apply when it is not wanted. Multiple table entries for a given
external-LO can used if necessary. A maximum of 40 table entries are
supported. A warning is given by the 'synth' command if an entry would apply
when the corresponding 'ifx' input is '1'.

Multiple table entries may cover a given total LO. The first in the table that
matches applies. Therefore you should enter the table with the most specific
entries before less specific ones. For example:

    ext_lo=
    ext_lo=loa,10500,usb,20000,20000
    ext_lo=loa,11000,usb,19000,21000

The monitor form 'ext_lo' will print the entire table. Using 'ext_lo=loX' with
no additional parameters will show the table entry that currently applies for
that IF (or all IFs for 'ext_lo=all'). This can be useful for debugging the
table. The output is all null parameters if none apply, including if the LO is
not defined.
